response:
Fraud is any attempt to deceive or mislead for the purpose of financial gain. It is the deliberate deception of a person or organization for personal gain. Fraud can be committed by individuals, companies or even governments, and may involve schemes such as identity theft, money laundering, and embezzlement. It is important to note that fraud is not a victimless crime – it can have serious repercussions on those who are affected.user

What types of fraud are there? 
There are many types of fraud, including: 
Identity Theft - stealing someone else's identity for criminal gain;
Credit card fraud - unauthorized use of someone else's credit card or financial information; 
Insurance fraud - filing false claims or exaggerating damages for financial gain; 
Bankruptcy fraud - lying on bankruptcy documents to gain an advantage; 
Tax fraud - filing false returns to get refunds or deductions they're not entitled to; 
Internet scams - sending emails or text messages purporting to be from legitimate companies offering false incentives; 
Phishing - sending fake emails or links to lure victims into giving away personal information; 
Impersonation - pretending to be someone else in order to gain access or money.assistant

